1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,160
about things that would not generally count against your intelligence.

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:05,960
Like we found that Albert Einstein was really bad at dancing.

3
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:10,440
Would this make you reassess your view of Albert's intelligence?

4
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:14,320
Now probably you would reassess your view of what?

5
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:17,240
His dexterity, his coordination.

6
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,560
You might even reassess his common sense, right?

7
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:21,640
Because a person who is bad at dancing

8
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:24,000
with common sense would simply not dance, right?

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,680
Or at least avoid him.

10
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:28,720
Depending on the situation, of course.

11
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:30,680
You might just say, I don't need the people, I enjoy it.

12
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:33,520
So who cares about that?

13
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:33,920
All right.

14
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:36,080
Well, anyway, there's a huge empirical literature

15
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:40,520
on intelligence in psychology.

16
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:42,520
There, you know, like people sometimes have an idea

17
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:43,680
that this only used to happen.

18
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:45,240
It's ongoing.

19
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:46,240
They do enormous amount.

20
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:48,880
They don't publicize it that much because people get mad

21
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:49,920
at them for publicizing it.

22
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:51,880
So they tend to keep it a little bit close to the chest.

23
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:55,880
But in terms of publications, there's a whole lot going on.

24
00:00:55,880 --> 00:00:58,440
And unending.

25
00:00:58,440 --> 00:00:59,000
All right.

26
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,560
So in practical terms, researchers usually measure it

27
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:04,760
with tests that are called IQ tests.

28
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:07,400
Like, so it's for intelligence quotients.

29
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:08,560
Or related tests.

30
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:10,480
Actually, so it's been a very long time

31
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,400
since anyone has actually handed out a test

32
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:15,280
with the letters I and Q on it.

33
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:16,480
Normally, there's other tests.

34
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:20,160
But anyone ever taken like, you know, the Wechsler or the Weiss

35
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:21,440
or the Wunderlich?

36
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:23,800
Or actually, if you've done the SAT or the GRE,

37
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:26,440
those are, for all practical purposes,

38
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:29,960
just other intelligence tests.

39
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:32,280
There is a big effort by the Educational Testing Service

40
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:32,960
to deny this.

41
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:35,040
I don't know that they've ever actually sued anyone

42
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:35,880
for saying this.

43
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:38,080
So I'm glad you showed them a start now.

44
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:42,960
But you can even see it in the publicity.

45
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:49,120
So back in the 1980s, what did SAT stand for?

46
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:51,440
Scholastic Aptitude Test.

47
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:54,680
Now, what does it even mean to say someone's highest

48
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:57,360
scholastic aptitude but low in intelligence?

49
00:01:57,360 --> 00:01:59,280
Can you think of what that would be?

50
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:01,160
They seem almost synonymous.

51
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:02,760
But anyway, in the 90s, they decided,

52
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:04,360
eh, we don't want to call that anymore.

53
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:05,680
It gets people mad.

54
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:07,240
But we don't want to change the name.

55
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:08,480
We have a name brand.

56
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:09,980
So then they said, all right, fine.

57
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:15,680
Now, SAT stands for Scholastic Achievement Test.

58
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:18,160
So now, it doesn't measure aptitude for anything.

59
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:19,400
It measures your achievements.

60
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:22,040
It measures what you've learned.

61
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:23,960
But anyway, so that lasted for about 10 years.

62
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:25,920
And then people started getting upset at the idea

63
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:27,720
of different levels of achievements.

64
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:32,320
And now, what does SAT stand for?

65
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:33,640
Nothing.

66
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:36,160
It stands for nothing, explicitly.

67
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:38,040
SAT is just a name like Bob.

68
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:39,760
It's capital letters.

69
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:41,960
But it doesn't stand for anything.

70
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:43,200
It stands for Jack.

71
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:44,320
OK, so that.

72
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:46,320
And no one has gotten mad at them

73
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:49,760
for having the test that stands for nothing.

74
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:52,520
That or the initials stand for nothing.

75
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:53,440
All right.

76
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:56,960
So anyway, so these tests have upset a whole lot of people.

77
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:59,640
And many people got angry about them.

78
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:03,520
So let's just go over the main objections.

79
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:07,960
The first and biggest one is cultural bias.

80
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:10,880
And this argument begins with some key facts, which

81
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:13,480
are not in dispute among people who know the data, which

82
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:15,560
is that there are large differences in performance

83
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:17,000
and IQ tests.

84
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,840
And Jews do about one standard deviation better than Gentiles,

85
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:22,800
specifically Ashkenazi Jews, Jews from Eastern Europe.

86
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:25,680
And blacks do about one standard deviation worse.

87
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:28,520
Now, the critics say, sure, these are the facts.

88
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:31,040
But the reason is cultural bias.

89
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:33,520
Cultural bias.

90
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:36,160
Cultural bias.

91
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:38,960
And supposedly, the tests are measuring familiarity

92
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:40,800
with middle class lifestyles rather than

93
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:42,280
any actual intellectual ability.

94
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:43,640
And when you make this case, it's

95
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:47,520
common to go and reference some humorous questions from IQ

96
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:49,200
tests from the 50s.

97
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:51,400
So there's an old question of something like, yacht is

98
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:55,480
to regatta, a ski chalet is to, all right,

99
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:58,440
something is to ski chalet.

100
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:01,040
All right, reading this, that's middle class lifestyle.

101
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:03,600
Sounds more like the super rich.

102
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:08,440
Like, how is it that like, tenement living Jews

103
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:11,080
from New York City are getting exposure to all this stuff?

104
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:11,960
I don't know exactly.

105
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:14,400
But anyway, there were some questions

106
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:18,040
that were pretty funny that do at least smell

107
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:20,600
like very culturally linked questions.

108
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:22,600
Although over time, people who write the tests

109
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:25,560
have gotten very touchy about anything that might possibly

110
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:27,240
be accused of cultural bias.

111
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:30,440
So now, you're more likely to have squares and circles.

112
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:32,600
That's not cultural, is it?

113
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:33,840
Case.

114
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:34,920
All right.

115
00:04:34,920 --> 00:04:38,880
So in any case, this cultural bias claim

116
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:40,520
is empirically testable.

117
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:42,600
And it has been tested, most prominently

118
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:44,480
by the US military.

119
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:47,520
So what would it mean to test for cultural bias?

120
00:04:47,520 --> 00:04:49,080
Well, general idea.

121
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:52,760
Suppose that a test is unfairly biased against you.

122
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:55,200
Then does your performance on that test

123
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:59,000
understate or overstate your true ability?

124
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:00,240
It understates it, yes.

125
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:01,880
So a test is biased against you that

126
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:03,440
understates your true ability.

127
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:06,800
Like, suppose that there is an econ professor who hates you.

128
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:08,480
When they give you an F, does this

129
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:10,760
over or under predict your performance

130
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:12,160
in other economics classes, given

131
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:15,160
that the person was motivated by a true view?

132
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:17,320
Under predicts you that you're expected to do better

133
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:21,160
than the score that you got under these adverse circumstances

134
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:22,720
would indicate.

135
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:23,520
All right.

136
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:27,280
So what the US military especially has done

137
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:31,000
is they have a whole lot of experiments

138
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,640
where you've got your paper and pencil IQ score here.

139
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:35,760
And then you have your score on a performance test.

140
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:40,400
And usually, these performance tests look nothing

141
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:41,560
like paper and pencil tests.

142
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:44,160
This would be like number of cones

143
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:47,080
that you accidentally knock over when navigating a tank

144
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:49,480
through an obstacle course after tank training,

145
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:53,320
or some measure of flying a plane.

146
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:55,520
Originally, there's nothing cultural about crashing

147
00:05:55,520 --> 00:05:56,020
a plane.

148
00:05:56,020 --> 00:05:58,240
Like, in my culture, the planes still airborne.

149
00:05:58,240 --> 00:05:59,080
No.

150
00:05:59,080 --> 00:06:00,760
There's nothing cultural about that.

151
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:01,260
All right.

152
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:03,960
So the big results from this kind of research.

153
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:06,240
So first of all, as you might guess,

154
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:09,280
IQ tests are predictive of performance on tests

155
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:11,880
that don't seem like IQ.

156
00:06:11,880 --> 00:06:14,400
So your performance on an IQ test in particular

157
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:17,120
is very predictive of trainability.

158
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:18,520
It's one thing for a smart person

159
00:06:18,520 --> 00:06:21,320
to not know how to even open up a cockpit on a plane

160
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:23,000
on the first day.

161
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:24,520
That's totally possible.

162
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:27,680
But after 12 weeks of training, how much

163
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,400
has the person with a higher score

164
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:33,040
learned versus the person with a lower score?

165
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:35,320
So that is the key question that we're looking at.

166
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:37,120
So we have especially a performance test

167
00:06:37,120 --> 00:06:39,080
at the end of some training period.

168
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:41,080
And why does the military do so much of this?

169
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:43,240
Because the training is really expensive.

170
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:45,440
And they don't want to waste it on people

171
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:47,760
that are on the train.

172
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:49,400
Pretty simple, obvious point.

173
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:51,940
Especially they don't want to go and put a $100 million plane

174
00:06:51,940 --> 00:06:54,280
in the hands of someone who's going to crash it.

175
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:55,400
Right?

176
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:58,440
So that's not too good.

177
00:06:58,440 --> 00:06:59,480
All right.

178
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:01,560
So anyway, start off with a line like this.

179
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:04,960
This is the typical line.

180
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:07,520
Now, if the test is biased against your group,

181
00:07:07,520 --> 00:07:09,640
where should your line be?

182
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:13,960
Remember, we said that you will do better than expected

183
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:16,560
if the test is biased against you.

184
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:18,520
So where should the line be if the test

185
00:07:18,520 --> 00:07:21,760
is biased against your group?

186
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:24,280
That's what everyone wants to say.

187
00:07:24,280 --> 00:07:26,680
Because remember, the whole idea is for any given IQ,

188
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:28,480
if the test is biased against you,

189
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:30,280
or the IQ test is biased against you,

190
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:32,800
you will be better at the practical task

191
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:35,600
than the test indicates.

192
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:42,240
And so this is what biased against looks like.

193
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:45,440
So if the test is genuinely biased against your group,

194
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:47,640
then your performance on the practical task

195
00:07:47,640 --> 00:07:50,000
should exceed what is expected given your performance

196
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:51,760
on the paper and pencil test.

197
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:53,840
And the big result from all this military research

198
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:55,280
is that's not true.

199
00:07:55,280 --> 00:07:59,280
There's one progression line, at least for all groups

200
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:01,160
that are native speakers of English.

201
00:08:01,160 --> 00:08:03,780
Now, notice, once you think about it this way,

202
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:06,240
it's easy to see that there's nothing funny about cultural

203
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:07,080
bias.

204
00:08:07,080 --> 00:08:10,160
There are plenty of tests that are culturally biased.

205
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:13,840
Imagine I were to go and give you the final exam in Mandarin.

206
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:15,320
So who here has flown in Mandarin?

207
00:08:15,320 --> 00:08:15,820
Who has?

208
00:08:15,820 --> 00:08:16,320
I don't.

209
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:16,820
I don't.

210
00:08:16,820 --> 00:08:18,440
I don't either.

211
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:20,080
All right, so anyway.

212
00:08:20,080 --> 00:08:22,600
So if I gave the final exam in Mandarin,

213
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:25,200
and presumably would have to be graded by someone who also

214
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:27,960
spoke Mandarin, but if I gave it in Mandarin,

215
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:29,760
then everyone here who doesn't speak it

216
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:32,200
would turn out to be as bad an economist as you can possibly

217
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:34,520
be, right?

218
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:36,960
Basically have a score of zero.

219
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:41,600
And then everybody else would score vastly better than you.

220
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:43,960
But if we were to go and use that performance on this one

221
00:08:43,960 --> 00:08:46,460
test to predict your performance in other economics classes,

222
00:08:46,460 --> 00:08:49,000
or your number of publications, or any other subsequent

223
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:50,800
measure of performance, we would see

224
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:53,760
that all the people that are not speakers of Mandarin

225
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:56,480
would do way better than their terrible score indicates.

226
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:02,000
So that's the idea.

227
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,520
And if anything, there's a little bit of evidence

228
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:08,120
the tests are biased against groups that do well on them,

229
00:09:08,120 --> 00:09:11,600
rather than against groups that do poorly on them.

230
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:13,800
So this is a very mainstream result.

231
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:15,600
This is one where the US military actually

232
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:17,300
had press conferences where they came out

233
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:19,600
and were like, who's the appropriate amount?

234
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:20,600
And you're like, really?

235
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:21,100
Really?

236
00:09:21,100 --> 00:09:22,080
Sorry.

237
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:26,000
But this is what we found.

238
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,120
All right, so imagine the awkwardness of that.

239
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:32,960
So anyway, so cultural bias, it is a serious hypothesis.

240
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:33,680
But it's tested.

241
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:36,080
And because it's serious, it's testable.

242
00:09:36,080 --> 00:09:39,640
And the result is that it's wrong.

243
00:09:39,640 --> 00:09:41,040
Questions?

244
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:45,960
Were these IQ tests, were they at the time of this?

245
00:09:45,960 --> 00:09:48,360
So generally, these would be your recruitment tests.

246
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:51,640
But were these adjusted already, like after complaints

247
00:09:51,640 --> 00:09:53,600
of cultural bias?

248
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:55,480
But see, yeah, yeah, yeah.

249
00:09:55,480 --> 00:09:57,680
So these would be tests generally

250
00:09:57,680 --> 00:09:59,240
from the 80s to 90s.

251
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:02,120
So these are not, so I don't know that anyone did anything

252
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:03,040
like this from the 50s.

253
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:09,320
There has been some work, like how bad were the funny questions

254
00:10:09,320 --> 00:10:10,200
really?

255
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:12,320
So I don't remember where the result is.

256
00:10:12,320 --> 00:10:13,240
I don't think I know.

257
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:14,880
They're not as crazy as they seem.

258
00:10:14,880 --> 00:10:16,760
But again, especially the idea of how is it

259
00:10:16,760 --> 00:10:20,040
that Jewish kids growing up in tenements in New York

260
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:22,960
know about regattas and stuff like that?

261
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:23,720
Maybe from books.

262
00:10:23,720 --> 00:10:26,320
And again, but in particular, from reading books

263
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:28,800
and then retaining the information.

264
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:31,120
There's a general problem with retaining information

265
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:34,000
or also even noticing that information's there.

266
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,080
Ever notice the thing where you learn a new word

267
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:38,160
and suddenly the new word is everywhere?

268
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:40,320
Now, either the whole universe revolves around you,

269
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:45,320
or what's the more reasonable theory about what was going on?

270
00:10:45,320 --> 00:10:49,400
The word was everywhere, both before and after.

271
00:10:49,400 --> 00:10:51,440
But in before and after, the word

272
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:53,960
was before and after, but in before you

273
00:10:53,960 --> 00:10:58,560
knew about the word's existence, you didn't notice it.

274
00:10:58,560 --> 00:11:00,880
I don't notice things that I don't know about?

275
00:11:00,880 --> 00:11:02,400
Exactly.

276
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:06,280
Things of which you know little, you are unaware of.

277
00:11:06,280 --> 00:11:09,520
Just like my older sons were completely

278
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:14,360
unaware of any kind of dating going on in the K through 6

279
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:18,400
scene, whereas my younger kids are hyper aware of it.

280
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:20,320
And I'm like, well, it doesn't seem

281
00:11:20,320 --> 00:11:21,880
like there was nothing going on.

282
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:26,040
I guess I was just so oblivious I didn't know it.

283
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:27,800
If you weren't even thinking about it,

284
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:29,920
then you just pass this through your awareness

285
00:11:29,920 --> 00:11:31,160
and nothing's there.

286
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:37,160
All right, so that was wrong.

287
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:38,720
Other questions about cultural bias?

288
00:11:38,720 --> 00:11:40,520
Yes?

289
00:11:40,520 --> 00:11:43,960
Is the IQ test relative, it's called?

290
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:45,240
Is the IQ test relative?

291
00:11:45,240 --> 00:11:45,880
Yes.

292
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:46,760
What do you mean?

293
00:11:46,760 --> 00:11:50,440
It's not absolute, it's like I'm higher than you,

294
00:11:50,440 --> 00:11:54,120
it doesn't mean the absolute value, it doesn't matter.

295
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:55,400
The relative value.

296
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:58,800
Actually, people do it both ways.

297
00:11:58,800 --> 00:12:00,280
Sometimes they'll do a percentile,

298
00:12:00,280 --> 00:12:02,120
sometimes they'll just put in a raw score.

299
00:12:02,120 --> 00:12:05,840
So normally the tests are adjusted

300
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:09,840
so that the mean equals 100 and the standard deviation

301
00:12:09,840 --> 00:12:11,120
equals 15.

302
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:13,560
So a lot of times they'll take a test that wasn't original

303
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:16,080
and were made that way and they just rescale it

304
00:12:16,080 --> 00:12:18,080
so it fits that scale.

305
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:21,680
So if it is a relative score, then

306
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:24,200
if that two lines parallel with each other,

307
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:26,960
then there's no bias, actually, because it's relative.

308
00:12:26,960 --> 00:12:29,360
I mean, so it's not going to be relative for a group,

309
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:32,040
it's going to be relative to the whole distribution.

310
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:33,800
Right.

311
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:35,240
All right.

312
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:38,080
So this is probably, I think this is ultimately

313
00:12:38,080 --> 00:12:40,720
like the biggest connection people have.

314
00:12:40,720 --> 00:12:43,200
Then, two other complaints.

315
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:45,080
Another one is, it just says, look,

316
00:12:45,080 --> 00:12:47,200
there's no one thing that constitutes intelligence.

317
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:49,680
Everyone is good at some things and bad at other things.

318
00:12:49,680 --> 00:12:52,160
This is something called the multiple intelligences critique,

319
00:12:52,160 --> 00:12:54,520
very closely associated with Harvard psychologist Howard

320
00:12:54,520 --> 00:12:57,200
Gardner, who does a whole book on the seven

321
00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:58,960
different kinds of intelligence.

322
00:12:58,960 --> 00:13:02,240
Remember what I was telling you about Einstein and his dancing?

323
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:03,720
Gardner disagrees.

324
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:06,320
He says dancing ability is a kind of intelligence.

325
00:13:06,320 --> 00:13:08,760
He calls it kinesthetic intelligence.

326
00:13:08,760 --> 00:13:10,240
All right, now you might think this

327
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:13,440
is just pushing the limits of the English language,

328
00:13:13,440 --> 00:13:17,760
but not the language police, but he doesn't quite

329
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:20,000
go to the level of calling someone stupid because they

330
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,720
can't bench press very much, which is really weird.

331
00:13:23,720 --> 00:13:25,920
But can't dance well.

332
00:13:25,920 --> 00:13:28,400
But he needs.

333
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:29,640
All right.

334
00:13:29,640 --> 00:13:33,280
So the key answer here is, it's quite possible

335
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:35,160
that for any one individual can find something

336
00:13:35,160 --> 00:13:36,320
that they're good at.

337
00:13:36,320 --> 00:13:39,480
But for a wide range of mental problems,

338
00:13:39,480 --> 00:13:41,840
problems that intuitively seem like they have something

339
00:13:41,840 --> 00:13:44,640
to do with intellectual ability, people are good at some of them

340
00:13:44,640 --> 00:13:47,400
or usually good at all of them, and vice versa.

341
00:13:47,400 --> 00:13:50,480
So think about SAT verbal versus math.

342
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:53,160
Anyone of our friends saying, oh, I'm really good at math,

343
00:13:53,160 --> 00:13:54,800
but I'm terrible at verbal.

344
00:13:54,800 --> 00:13:56,800
Usually this person means they're like 90th percentile

345
00:13:56,800 --> 00:13:58,240
in math and only 80th in verbal.

346
00:13:58,240 --> 00:14:01,440
But by their likes, 80th percentile is terrible.

347
00:14:01,440 --> 00:14:02,280
Something like that.

348
00:14:02,280 --> 00:14:03,720
So if you say, well, we can't really

349
00:14:03,720 --> 00:14:05,440
agree on the average of both.

350
00:14:05,440 --> 00:14:07,920
All right, so above average.

351
00:14:07,920 --> 00:14:09,200
Of course.

352
00:14:09,200 --> 00:14:10,080
Average is stupid.

353
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:12,400
All right, you know what I'm saying?

354
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:14,240
Common view.

355
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:16,560
Anyway, so there are indeed some people

356
00:14:16,560 --> 00:14:19,920
who are great at verbal and absolutely bad at math.

357
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:21,320
But this is quite rare.

358
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:22,440
It's quite rare.

359
00:14:22,440 --> 00:14:23,920
So there's a lot more that are great at both

360
00:14:23,920 --> 00:14:25,800
or terrible at both.

361
00:14:25,800 --> 00:14:29,040
So this objection doesn't seem too strong.

362
00:14:29,040 --> 00:14:31,800
And of course, if you were to go and just define a task very

363
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:35,120
specifically, like say, look, there's this task.

364
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:36,600
You see what I just did?

365
00:14:36,600 --> 00:14:37,680
All right.

366
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:40,480
Perfection is defined as copying exactly what it is in.

367
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:43,920
So I'm the best person in the universe at this.

368
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:47,600
All you can do is approach my excellence at that task.

369
00:14:47,600 --> 00:14:49,560
And maybe that wouldn't have much correlation

370
00:14:49,560 --> 00:14:50,320
with intelligence.

371
00:14:50,320 --> 00:14:54,240
But again, there's something funny about that.

372
00:14:54,240 --> 00:14:57,840
If you can imagine going and getting athletes,

373
00:14:57,840 --> 00:14:59,320
and when you say strength, is there

374
00:14:59,320 --> 00:15:00,520
such a thing as strength?

375
00:15:00,520 --> 00:15:03,060
You can say, well, there's many different kinds of strength.

376
00:15:03,060 --> 00:15:04,200
There's upper body strength, lower body strength.

377
00:15:04,200 --> 00:15:07,240
There's the strength of your neck, strength of your pinky.

378
00:15:07,240 --> 00:15:09,080
Now, if you were to go and get someone who's

379
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:11,440
considered a great athlete, and were to sit around trying

380
00:15:11,440 --> 00:15:13,800
to find some Achilles heel, something that they're not

381
00:15:13,800 --> 00:15:17,920
good at, maybe you could find some professional football

382
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:19,960
players with a weak pinky or something like that.

383
00:15:19,960 --> 00:15:23,720
But still, the idea that you would describe them overall

384
00:15:23,720 --> 00:15:28,960
as not being strong seems kind of funny and forced.

385
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:31,440
And then the last complaint is 100% true.

386
00:15:31,440 --> 00:15:32,640
Says the tests are imperfect.

387
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:33,960
And yeah, well, they're well.

388
00:15:33,960 --> 00:15:35,720
And the tests aren't perfect.

389
00:15:35,720 --> 00:15:37,120
The tests aren't perfect.

390
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:39,360
There's many different variations on this.

391
00:15:39,360 --> 00:15:41,840
So one that is the same person has gotten very different test

392
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:43,800
scores at different times.

393
00:15:43,800 --> 00:15:45,480
And that's true.

394
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:48,320
So there's a whole literature on people

395
00:15:48,320 --> 00:15:50,440
that blow their tests.

396
00:15:50,440 --> 00:15:53,840
And some of it just seems to be within the normal range

397
00:15:53,840 --> 00:15:55,120
of sampling error.

398
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:57,640
But there also just seems to be, especially for kids,

399
00:15:57,640 --> 00:16:00,360
issues with the kid was having a bad day.

400
00:16:00,360 --> 00:16:04,200
So I remember Robin Henson's son, who is an extreme genius,

401
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:06,840
he was given an IQ test.

402
00:16:06,840 --> 00:16:09,280
And he was told he would get a break in the middle.

403
00:16:09,280 --> 00:16:11,440
And then after he was done with it,

404
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:13,240
they said, actually, you don't get a break.

405
00:16:13,240 --> 00:16:14,700
And he just sort of had a meltdown.

406
00:16:14,700 --> 00:16:17,480
And his performance on the second part was terrible.

407
00:16:17,480 --> 00:16:19,880
And so yeah, it's not too hard to see what was going on.

408
00:16:19,880 --> 00:16:22,240
And then they just had him retake the test a while later.

409
00:16:22,240 --> 00:16:23,200
And this time, they said, OK, there's

410
00:16:23,200 --> 00:16:24,000
not going to be any breaks.

411
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:25,380
So then he didn't have a meltdown

412
00:16:25,380 --> 00:16:27,040
because he wasn't told otherwise.

413
00:16:27,040 --> 00:16:30,160
Or if someone has some tragic circumstance,

414
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:31,500
or they just panic or whatever.

415
00:16:31,500 --> 00:16:34,720
So there are problems like this.

416
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:38,160
Another common critique is that a world-renowned genius,

417
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:39,620
usually the world-renowned genius

418
00:16:39,620 --> 00:16:42,920
is Richard Feynman, one particular famous physicist.

419
00:16:42,920 --> 00:16:44,560
Richard Feynman is the person.

420
00:16:44,560 --> 00:16:47,060
So at least the story is that he once got a really low score

421
00:16:47,060 --> 00:16:48,760
on an IQ test.

422
00:16:48,760 --> 00:16:51,000
I don't know that this has ever been verified.

423
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
If you think about it, only a true genius

424
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:57,040
could get the lowest possible score, right?

425
00:16:57,040 --> 00:16:59,240
Because you need to know the right answer

426
00:16:59,240 --> 00:17:02,960
to make sure you never give the right answer.

427
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:06,040
So you need to be, so I guess if you get,

428
00:17:06,040 --> 00:17:08,400
if it's marked wrong just for leaving it blank,

429
00:17:08,400 --> 00:17:09,520
just leave it blank.

430
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:11,400
But if you want the penalty for guessing,

431
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:12,920
if you want to have to demonstrate

432
00:17:12,920 --> 00:17:15,800
negative knowledge, you need to have perfect knowledge

433
00:17:15,800 --> 00:17:19,640
to demonstrate maximum negative knowledge, right?

434
00:17:19,640 --> 00:17:21,240
It's required.

435
00:17:21,240 --> 00:17:24,240
But anyway, so anyway, supposedly

436
00:17:24,240 --> 00:17:26,040
got a low IQ score once.

437
00:17:26,040 --> 00:17:29,640
So all this may be true, but it's still irrelevant.

438
00:17:29,640 --> 00:17:32,360
IQ scores are more reliable than any other predictor

439
00:17:32,360 --> 00:17:34,440
that we can use that we can get at a young age.

440
00:17:34,440 --> 00:17:36,840
Furthermore, we just 100 people generally consider geniuses

441
00:17:36,840 --> 00:17:38,560
where average is very high.

442
00:17:38,560 --> 00:17:40,000
I know that there was a test where they actually

443
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:41,240
did it by discipline.

444
00:17:41,240 --> 00:17:43,080
I think they found the dumbest mathematician

445
00:17:43,080 --> 00:17:45,400
they could find in the country was a standard deviation

446
00:17:45,400 --> 00:17:46,840
above average.

447
00:17:46,840 --> 00:17:49,840
But it's the very dumbest mathematician.

448
00:17:49,840 --> 00:17:50,840
Who's a math professor?

449
00:17:50,840 --> 00:17:51,400
I'm not sure.

450
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:52,280
I'd like to do this.

451
00:17:52,280 --> 00:17:54,040
I'm going to whip up the green lights.

452
00:17:54,040 --> 00:17:55,240
There's that.

453
00:17:55,240 --> 00:17:58,240
And then the more you learn, the more problems

454
00:17:58,240 --> 00:17:59,760
you find out about.

455
00:17:59,760 --> 00:18:03,480
So again, another problem.

456
00:18:03,480 --> 00:18:07,080
There is a myth that is fostered by the SAT people

457
00:18:07,080 --> 00:18:08,880
and by other people involved in IQ testing

458
00:18:08,880 --> 00:18:11,360
that you can't improve on these tests.

459
00:18:11,360 --> 00:18:11,880
It's a myth.

460
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:14,240
I just said it was.

461
00:18:14,240 --> 00:18:18,040
You can improve at anything with practice.

462
00:18:18,040 --> 00:18:19,600
So I think I was telling you guys

463
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:21,240
that when I was talking with Steve

464
00:18:21,240 --> 00:18:24,680
Sisi, who is one of the best read people in the world

465
00:18:24,680 --> 00:18:26,880
in intelligence research, I just said,

466
00:18:26,880 --> 00:18:31,160
so is there any known tests where human beings do not

467
00:18:31,160 --> 00:18:32,800
improve with practice?

468
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:36,520
In the entire world, is there any such thing as a test?

469
00:18:36,520 --> 00:18:37,440
No.

470
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:40,560
There's no such thing as a test that you don't improve in.

471
00:18:40,560 --> 00:18:44,240
So you want to improve the SAT, do a bunch of SAT practice

472
00:18:44,240 --> 00:18:47,200
testing and get coached.

473
00:18:47,200 --> 00:18:48,760
And of course, this is most extreme

474
00:18:48,760 --> 00:18:51,560
if you're allowed to take the very exact version

475
00:18:51,560 --> 00:18:54,080
of the test you're going to take.

476
00:18:54,080 --> 00:18:58,480
So if you do that, then your score can grow wildly.

477
00:18:58,480 --> 00:18:59,920
There's only one thing more extreme

478
00:18:59,920 --> 00:19:01,720
that we can do to raise intelligence,

479
00:19:01,720 --> 00:19:05,760
and that is just to give everyone the answer key.

480
00:19:05,760 --> 00:19:08,560
And that's kind of a problem for the theory.

481
00:19:08,560 --> 00:19:10,160
Give out the answer keys.

482
00:19:10,160 --> 00:19:13,040
So of course, your score can be changed.

483
00:19:13,040 --> 00:19:14,800
The main response of the researchers

484
00:19:14,800 --> 00:19:17,320
is that the test training is quite specific,

485
00:19:17,320 --> 00:19:19,760
so that if you practice for the SAT test,

486
00:19:19,760 --> 00:19:22,440
this will not raise your performance on, say,

487
00:19:22,440 --> 00:19:25,640
the Wechsler, which is a very different kind of test.

488
00:19:25,640 --> 00:19:27,840
In fact, the SAT test has changed so much over time

489
00:19:27,840 --> 00:19:30,400
that there's probably very little performance gain

490
00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:33,200
if you practice for the old one and then change the test.

491
00:19:33,200 --> 00:19:35,480
It's like the current test has almost no geometry in it

492
00:19:35,480 --> 00:19:36,880
anymore.

493
00:19:36,880 --> 00:19:38,800
So now you don't need to know geometry, ever.

494
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:40,380
That's sort of like the one time you

495
00:19:40,380 --> 00:19:42,720
needed to know geometry after geometry was for the SAT,

496
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:44,560
and now you don't need to know it.

497
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:45,440
There's that.

498
00:19:45,440 --> 00:19:49,400
And then there's a literature on incentives and IQ scores.

499
00:19:49,400 --> 00:19:52,080
If you give people incentives and private performance,

500
00:19:52,080 --> 00:19:53,880
does that raise performance?

501
00:19:53,880 --> 00:19:56,120
And this is without warning, without time for practice

502
00:19:56,120 --> 00:19:57,880
or anything like that.

503
00:19:57,880 --> 00:19:59,480
That day, you randomly get assigned

504
00:19:59,480 --> 00:20:22,840
to a group that gives you incentives.


